title: Macros
tags: Commands
kind: guide
summary: F3 records the commands you run from the keyboard, F4 runs them again; macro:ask makes an answer asked at each run, and macro:save keeps the macro as a user command in macros.js.


A macro is a sequence of commands recorded as you run them, to run again in one go —
as in Emacs, and on the same keys.

|!Key |!Command |!Does |
|<<key "macro:toggle-recording" f3>> |`macro:toggle-recording` |starts recording; pressed again, stops and keeps what was recorded as ''the'' macro |
|<<key "macro:run" f4>> |`macro:run` |runs the macro |
|<<key "macro:ask" "ctrl+g">> |`macro:ask` |in a question, while recording: that argument is asked again at each run |
| |`macro:save <name>` |keeps the macro as the command `user:<name>`, in `macros.js` |

While recording, every status bar shows ''● recording''.

!! What is recorded

The commands you run ''from the keyboard'' — a key, or a line of the command input,
`!` shell lines included — each with the arguments it ran with, those it asked for
too. Nothing else:

* not a click: a button runs a command, but it often does something first that is no command (the ↗ of a list row selects the row, then opens it), so recording some clicks and not others would make what a macro holds a guess. To record what a button does, run its command (every control has one: [[Commands]]);
* not what you type into a panel's own text field — the list's finder, a form: the typing is no command;
* not what a script sends ([[mf gui command]]), nor what a command runs itself (a user command's `mf.invoke`): those are its doing, and run again with it;
* not a command that failed, nor one abandoned at a question with `Escape`;
* not the keystroke-level commands, those kept out of the message log (`editing:*`, `completion:cycle`, `find:*`, `help*`…), nor the `macro:*` ones.

What a step acts on is decided when it runs, as when you type it: the focused
workspace and slot, the selection (`metarecord:bulk selection …`), the panel the
cursor is in. A macro recorded on one row runs on the row you are on.

A recording that recorded nothing leaves the macro of before in place. The macro
lasts for the session; `macro:save` keeps it.

!! Asked again at each run

A question answered while recording is answered the same way at each run. Press
<<key "macro:ask" "ctrl+g">> while the question is on screen, then answer it: that
argument will be asked at each run instead — its own question, its own candidates. A
second press takes it back. Recording `metarecord:bulk selection set` and marking the
value gives a macro that asks for a new value and sets it.

!! Running

`macro:run` runs the steps in order, each once the one before has finished — what it
opened shown and loaded ([[When a command has finished]]). It stops at the first step
that fails, saying which (''macro stopped at step 2 of 3 (…)''), and at a question
abandoned with `Escape`. Everything the macro writes to the repository is one label
of the log, under which each step's is nested: `log:undo` undoes it all, or offers
the step ([[Undo and redo by label]]).

!! Saving

`macro:save rate-all` writes the macro at the end of `gui/macros.js` as the user
command `user:rate-all`, and loads it: it is a command like any other, listed,
bindable to a key, runnable by a script. A name taken by a command, or already in
the file, is refused.

```
  'user:rate-all': {
    label: 'Macro user:rate-all',
    run: async (mf) => {
      await mf.invoke(['metarecord-list:select', 'all']);
      await mf.invoke(['metarecord:bulk', 'selection', 'set', 'rating', mf.ask]);
    },
  },
```

The entry is what one would write by hand in `user-commands.js` ([[User commands]]):
read it to see how, and edit it as you like — a condition, a loop, a step removed.
The GUI never rewrites the file: it adds each new entry before the `};` that closes
it, and refuses to save when the file no longer ends that way. After editing it by
hand, `config:reload commands`. An entry named like one of `user-commands.js` is left
out, the first one standing.

!! Repeating

There is no count: running a macro N times, or until it fails, would be a repetition
of any command, not of macros alone, and is left to that.
